  "unit": "latin",
  "title": "Believe, Trust, True and Know: cred, fid, ver and sci",
  "short": "cred means believe, fid means trust, ver means true and sci means know.",
  "trouble": "fid shrinks in diffident (dis + fid), and ver hides in verdict. credulous (too ready to believe) and incredulous (not believing) are near opposites.",
  "prereqs": [
    "roots.dict-loqu"
  ],
  "xp": 1,
  "viz": [
    "vo-roots",
    "vo-word"
  ],
  "figureArchetype": "GeneralFigure",
  "practice": null,
  "lesson": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "Belief and truth have their own roots. <b>cred</b> means “believe”: a story that is <i>incredible</i> is hard to believe, and your <i>credit</i> is how far people believe you will pay. <b>fid</b> means “trust” or “faith”: to <i>confide</i> is to trust someone with a secret. <b>ver</b> means “true” (<i>very</i> once meant “truly”). <b>sci</b> means “know”: <i>science</i> is knowledge."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-scene",
        "opts": {
